      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND AND AIMS",
            "content": "The role of body mass index (BMI) for the prognosis of heart failure (HF) patients remains to be a great interest for a long time. However, the precise effect of dose-response for BMI and mortality risk in patients with HF is still unclear. We conducted a dose-response meta-analysis to quantitatively assess the effect of BMI on all-cause mortality in subjects with HF."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Fourteen prospective cohort studies included 13 508 death cases identified among 46 794 patients with HF."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The summary HR per 5 units increase in BMI was 0.95 (95% CI=0.92-0.97) with evidence of high heterogeneity (I(2) =90.10%, Pheterogeneity <.00001), and an U-shaped nonlinear relationship of BMI and risk of mortality from all-causes was revealed (Pnonlinearity =.0025). When stratified analyses were conducted, the heterogeneity in patients with HF aged more than 60 years decreased."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Heart failure patients with higher BMI (>28 kg/m(2) ) have a better survival, whereas underweight and those with severe obesity (eg, BMI>37 kg/m(2) ) have a higher mortality."
          }
        ]
      },
